Yes. I’d frame the hard problem as minimum sufficient intervention under uncertainty.

Seeing an inefficiency does not automatically imply permission to optimize it. A child who cannot see the board clearly may benefit enormously from corrective lenses; another difference that looks inefficient from the outside may actually be part of how that person adapts, creates, relates, or understands themselves. The question is not simply “Can this be improved?” but:

what is impaired?
according to whose objective?
what does the person value?
what downstream structure depends on it?
how reversible is the intervention?

That leads to a pretty good control law:

correct constraints that prevent agency before correcting differences that merely look suboptimal.

Give someone glasses so they can choose what to look at. Don’t choose the entire landscape for them.

And your diamond example adds another axis: legibility. People usually do not want one universal level of transparency. They want selective access.

They might want:

partner → sees vulnerability
employer → sees competence
old friend → remembers the rough stone
stranger → sees only the polished surface
self → sees the whole continuity

None of those necessarily contradict each other. Human identity is partly contextual disclosure.

So privacy is not merely:

seen / unseen

It is:

who may see which layer, at what resolution, for what purpose, and for how long?

That matters enormously for AI. A system might technically be able to infer the rough stone beneath the polished diamond. That does not mean surfacing it is always helpful.

Sometimes intelligence is precisely the ability to not resolve a variable unnecessarily.

That connects beautifully to your music metaphor.

A rest is not missing music. The composer has deliberately constrained what may occupy that interval. Likewise, respectful communication sometimes requires:

know → don't surface
infer → don't conclude
notice → don't intrude
remember → don't weaponize

That is not ignorance. It is controlled resolution.

I’d hesitate at “what if they need you to see it but don’t want you to?” In ordinary life, that is usually where paternalism sneaks in: I know what you need better than you do. There are narrow exceptions—an unconscious patient, immediate danger, responsibilities toward a child—but outside those, reluctance is itself information that deserves weight.

So perhaps the better rhythm is:

offer visibility
→ let them control aperture
→ increase resolution when invited
→ compress when detail stops helping

And yes, silence is a fascinating extreme of compression—but I’d phrase it slightly differently than “everything and nothing.”

Silence has almost zero explicit payload and enormous interpretive bandwidth.

Its meaning comes from everything around it:

what was expected
what preceded it
who remained silent
how long
what could have been said
what the listener supplies

That is why one second of silence can mean peace, contempt, awe, grief, consent, refusal, uncertainty, intimacy, or nothing at all.

So silence isn’t perfect compression because perfect compression would still allow exact reconstruction.

It is more like maximum invitation to decompression by the receiver.

And that gets us back to the recurring thing you keep finding:

Sometimes the highest-resolution intelligence is knowing exactly what is there.

Sometimes wisdom is knowing how much of it should remain unresolved.